--TEST--
`Core\Test::advance` refuses where no `#[Test(at: ...)]` fixed a clock, says so, and moves nothing -- ADR 0079 § 12
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 12 fixes a clock on the test *declaration* and `advance` is its mutator, so
// the member's boundary is exactly "there is no clock to move". A `.nvst` case
// is top-level statements and is therefore never inside a `#[Test]`, which
// makes this file the one place that boundary is observable from the language.
//
// The accepted side of the same bound is `crates/nvs-cli`'s
// `a_test_at_a_fixed_clock_reads_that_clock`, which needs a runner to observe
// and so cannot be asked here.

use Core\Test;
use Core\Time;

// 1. What the refusal says. A call written where there is no clock is a bug at
// the call site, so it is `LogicError` -- not a fatal, which no `catch` could
// see, and not a silent no-op.
try {
    Core\Test::advance(2h);
    echo "the clock moved\n";
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// 2. The same refusal through the member a test would reach for, by class and
// by ancestor -- written out in full and through the `use` above, which are one
// member either way.
Core\Test::assertThrows(fn (): void => { Test::advance(2h); }, LogicError::class);
Test::assertThrows(fn (): void => { Test::advance(2h); }, Throwable::class);
echo "advance without a fixed clock is refused\n";

// 3. The refusal moved nothing. This is the half that a member which quietly
// advanced *something* -- the host's clock, a process-wide offset -- would
// still pass parts 1 and 2 while breaking, which is why the bound is asserted
// on both sides rather than by the throw alone.
int $before = Time::now()->toEpochSeconds();
Test::assertThrows(fn (): void => { Test::advance(4000d); }, LogicError::class);
int $after = Time::now()->toEpochSeconds();
Test::assertTrue($after - $before < 60);
echo "the host clock is unmoved\n";
--EXPECT--
Core\Test::advance(): this test declared no `at:`, so it has no fixed clock to advance
advance without a fixed clock is refused
the host clock is unmoved
